---
// Accessibility statement. Static page — prerender to HTML served from the CDN
// (output: 'server' is on-demand by default).
//
// EVERY CLAIM ON THIS PAGE MUST BE BACKED BY SOMETHING THAT RUNS. That is the
// whole point of writing it: an accessibility statement is a public commitment,
// and one that drifts out of date is worse than none. Each specific assertion
// below maps to an automated check that fails the build or the test suite if it
// stops being true:
//   - "no axe-core violations"          -> npm run check:a11y (gates CI)
//   - contrast across every palette      -> test/themeContrast.test.ts
//   - pass/fail never colour-hue alone   -> test/drillStateColors.test.ts
//   - keyboard on every segmented switch -> src/scripts/radiogroupKeys.ts
// If you change any of those, update this page in the same commit.
//
// The conformance wording is deliberately "substantially conforms" and NOT
// "fully conformant". A formal full-conformance claim rests on human evaluation
// with real assistive technology, which has not been done here — and the known
// limitations section says so plainly rather than quietly omitting it.
export const prerender = true;
import { css } from 'styled-system/css';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { SOCIALS } from '../consts';
import { prosePara, proseH2, proseLink } from '../styles/prose';

const para = prosePara;
const h2 = proseH2;
const list = css({ color: 'textMuted', lineHeight: 1.9, pl: '1.25rem', mb: '1rem' });
const link = proseLink;
const strongText = css({ color: 'text' });
const inlineCode = css({ fontFamily: 'mono', fontSize: '0.9em' });

// Brand fork (CLAUDE.md invariant 14): the site is the same build on both
// domains, so every accessibility claim holds identically. Only the sentence
// that names a domain is forked. FLAT single-level selector — never nested.
const hideOnCyberkit = css({ '[data-brand="cyberkit"] &': { display: 'none' } });
const onlyOnCyberkit = css({ display: 'none', '[data-brand="cyberkit"] &': { display: 'inline' } });

const updated = 'July 31, 2026';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Accessibility"
  description="How this site approaches accessibility — target conformance, what is verified automatically on every build, known limitations, and how to report a problem."
>
  <PageHeader
    breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Accessibility' }]}
    eyebrow="Accessibility"
    title="Accessibility statement"
    description="What this site aims for, what is actually checked on every build, where it falls short, and how to tell me if something doesn't work for you."
  />

  <p class={para}>
    I want this site to be usable by everyone who wants to read it — including with a keyboard
    only, with a screen reader, at high zoom, or with motion reduced. This page sets out what that
    means in practice and, more usefully, <strong class={strongText}>which parts of it are
    enforced automatically</strong> rather than merely intended.
  </p>

  <h2 class={h2}>Conformance target</h2>
  <p class={para}>
    The target is <strong class={strongText}>WCAG 2.2 Level AA</strong>. This site
    <strong class={strongText}> substantially conforms</strong> to it: the automated and structural
    criteria below are verified on every build, and the interaction patterns have been checked by
    hand. I have deliberately not claimed <em>full</em> conformance — that rests on evaluation with
    real assistive technology, which I have not yet done. See the limitations below; I would rather
    understate this and be accurate than claim a level I cannot evidence.
  </p>

  <h2 class={h2}>What is verified on every build</h2>
  <p class={para}>
    These aren't aspirations — each one fails the build or the test suite if it stops being true:
  </p>
  <ul class={list}>
    <li>
      <strong class={strongText}>No automated violations.</strong> Every page template is scanned
      with <a class={link} href="https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core" target="_blank" rel="noopener">axe-core</a>{' '}
      and the build fails on any finding — currently zero across all page types.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class={strongText}>Colour contrast, on every theme.</strong> All ten colour palettes
      are checked programmatically: every text colour against every surface it can appear on clears
      the 4.5:1 AA threshold, and interactive borders clear 3:1. Changing a palette without meeting
      that fails the tests.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class={strongText}>Colour is never the only signal.</strong> Pass/fail and
      added/removed states carry a marker, an icon or text as well as a colour, and a test enforces
      that the two states also differ in hue rather than only in shade.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class={strongText}>Keyboard operability.</strong> Every custom control — the
      segmented switches, the ⌘K search palette, the sidebar, collapsible sections and the practice
      drills — is reachable and operable by keyboard alone, with arrow-key navigation and a single
      tab stop on grouped controls, per the WAI-ARIA patterns.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class={strongText}>It works without JavaScript.</strong> Every page renders its full
      content with scripts disabled. Animations and enhancements are layered on top; none of them
      hide content by default.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class={strongText}>Reduced motion is honoured.</strong> With{' '}
      <code class={inlineCode}>prefers-reduced-motion</code> set, every animation settles
      immediately to its final, visible state — nothing loops, decodes or moves.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class={strongText}>Structure.</strong> One <code class={inlineCode}>h1</code> per
      page, no skipped heading levels, landmark regions covering all content, and a skip link to
      the main content.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class={h2}>Known limitations</h2>
  <p class={para}>
    Being specific here is more useful than a blanket assurance:
  </p>
  <ul class={list}>
    <li>
      <strong class={strongText}>No assistive-technology testing yet.</strong> The checks above are
      automated and manual-by-inspection. Nobody has yet driven this site end to end with VoiceOver,
      NVDA or JAWS. Automated tooling catches roughly half of real accessibility problems, so this
      is the most significant gap and the next thing I intend to close.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class={strongText}>Automated scanning samples page templates.</strong> The site has
      over 1,700 pages generated from a small number of templates; the scan covers every template
      and every hand-written page rather than all pages individually.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class={strongText}>Some tools are inherently visual.</strong> The interactive
      analysis tools present dense tabular output. They are keyboard-operable and announce their
      results, but a few — the diff viewer especially — are harder to follow non-visually than I
      would like.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class={strongText}>Third-party content.</strong> Some pages embed data generated from
      external sources such as MITRE ATT&amp;CK and D3FEND. I control the presentation, not the
      source wording.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class={h2}>Tell me if something doesn't work</h2>
  <p class={para}>
    If any part of this site is difficult or impossible for you to use, I'd genuinely like to know —
    a specific report ("the X control can't be reached with a keyboard", "the Y result isn't
    announced") is worth far more to me than the automated scan. Reach me on{' '}
    <a class={link} href={SOCIALS.linkedin} target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>{' '}
    <span class={hideOnCyberkit}>
      or open an issue on <a class={link} href={SOCIALS.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>
    </span>
    <span class={onlyOnCyberkit}>
      or via the <a class={link} href="/colophon/">colophon</a>
    </span>, and please say which browser and assistive technology you were using. There is no
    third party to escalate to here — this is a personal site, so it comes to me and I will fix it.
  </p>

  <p class={css({ color: 'textMuted', fontSize: '0.85rem', mt: '2rem' })}>
    Last reviewed {updated}. This statement describes the site as built at that date; the
    automated checks referenced above run on every deploy.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
